### Requests for the ASP.NET Core sample. Start it with:
###   dotnet run --project samples/RuleLoom.Samples.AspNetCore
### The demo page at http://localhost:5080 does the same with buttons, and shows what the rules did.
###
### The scheduling demo: place Ada's and Bob's orders, then pay Ada's. Thirty seconds after placing them the payment
### reminders are due: Bob's is sent, Ada's is skipped because the rule no longer matches her order as it is then.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5080

### Ada (gold) places an order of 640.50 with 5 items: "VIP order" emails her, "Payment reminder" is scheduled 30 seconds
### before payment is due (in 60 seconds), "Big basket" and "Loyalty check" (her earlier orders) run too.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{ "customerEmail": "ada@example.com", "total": 640.50, "itemCount": 5 }

### Bob (bronze) places an order of 75.00: only "Payment reminder" matches.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{ "customerEmail": "bob@example.com", "total": 75.00 }

### Ada pays (SO-1001): "Order paid" emails her; her pending reminder will be skipped when it's due.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/SO-1001/pay

### Ada's order ships: "Order shipped" emails her now and asks for a review 20 seconds later.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/SO-1001/ship

### An order that comes in late: payment is due in 15 seconds, so its reminder was due 15 seconds ago, more than the
### 10-second grace period: skipped at once. With 25 seconds it's 5 seconds late, and sent at once.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{ "customerEmail": "bob@example.com", "total": 75.00, "paymentDueInSeconds": 15 }

### The orders, the emails sent, what the rules did, and the rules as loaded (with the ones left out for mistakes).
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/demo/emails

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/demo/activity

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/demo/rules

### The catalog document: events and their contexts, context schemas, operators and actions (with their parameters),
### as a rule-authoring UI reads them. Mapped by MapRuleLoom("/rules").
GET {{baseUrl}}/rules/catalog

### Validate a rule before saving it (MapRuleLoom). Valid: "to" is a path into the customer context.
POST {{baseUrl}}/rules/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": "welcome-gold",
  "name": "Welcome gold customers",
  "eventType": "OrderPlaced",
  "condition": { "operator": "eq", "operands": [{ "path": "@customer.Tier" }, { "value": "gold" }] },
  "actions": [{ "action": "send-email", "parameters": { "to": { "$path": "@customer.Email" }, "subject": "Welcome back" } }]
}

### Validate: parameter errors ("cc" isn't a parameter, "to" is missing) and a warning (the subject is a path to a number).
POST {{baseUrl}}/rules/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": "order-total",
  "name": "Order total",
  "eventType": "OrderPlaced",
  "condition": { "operator": "gte", "operands": [{ "path": "Total" }, { "value": 100 }] },
  "actions": [{ "action": "send-email", "parameters": { "cc": "sales@example.com", "subject": { "$path": "Total" } } }]
}

### Validate an array, rule by rule: the first rule is valid, the second has no condition (malformed), the third uses an
### unknown field. Each result has its index, and paths relative to its rule.
POST {{baseUrl}}/rules/validate
Content-Type: application/json

[
  { "id": "big-basket", "name": "Big basket", "eventType": "OrderPlaced",
    "condition": { "operator": "gte", "operands": [{ "path": "ItemCount" }, { "value": 5 }] } },
  { "id": "no-condition", "name": "No condition", "eventType": "OrderPlaced" },
  { "id": "typo", "name": "Typo", "eventType": "OrderPlaced",
    "condition": { "operator": "gte", "operands": [{ "path": "Totl" }, { "value": 5 }] } }
]
